# Loanword speakers — write now, scan later

When prose uses a word from another language, the reader should see the
home spelling and — **only if we have a verified human recording** — a
speaker. Do this in the **same change** as the article or catalog entry.
A periodic scan exists because mentions still slip through.

Sourcing catalog (where to look, what’s shipped, what’s ruled out):
`.cursor/rules/pronunciation-audio.mdc`. House style:
`.cursor/rules/history-articles.mdc`.

## Hard rule — human audio or no speaker

**A real human saying the matching word and sense, openly licensed, or
nothing.** Never `speechSynthesis`, never a TTS service, never a generated
voice, never a “close enough” language (German for Yiddish). A registered
word with no clip shows native script and **no speaker**. That silence is
correct. Never invent a URL or ship audio you did not verify.

## At write time (every new / expanded article or entry)

1. **Name it → register it.** One `SpokenWord` in `src/lib/words/registry.ts`.
   Reuse an existing `id`. Attested native spelling only. Same `id` as the
   concept slug when it is also a `<Term>`.
2. **First meaningful mention only.** Hand-authored TSX: `<Term id>` if it
   is a concept, else `<Word id="…">word</Word>`. Keep both **out of
   quotations** (`<Term>` may go *inside* a quote; `<Word>` may not).
3. **Hunt a clip** per `pronunciation-audio.mdc`. Verify human + matching
   sense + redistributable license, then self-host + credit. No clip →
   omit `audio` and log the miss in that file.
4. **Registry strings** (`about`, `answer`, `micro`, place `blurb`) cannot
   hold JSX. Add precise `aliases` and confirm the page calls
   `makeTermLinker()`. One linker per page.

```tsx
// ❌ BAD — hanging transliteration, or a fake voice
the little sobbing <em>krechtz</em>
utterance.text = "krechtz"; speechSynthesis.speak(utterance);

// ✅ GOOD — concept loanword (native + speaker if a clip exists)
the little sobbing <Term id="krechtz">krechtz</Term>

// ✅ GOOD — not a concept
<Word id="freylekhs">freylekhs</Word>
```

English song titles, artist names, and place names are not loanwords.
A Yiddish *song title* used as a common noun (*dos kelbl*) is.

## Periodic scan (run when asked, or every few content drops)

### What to look for

| Hit | Why it matters |
|---|---|
| `<em>foreign</em>` / `*italic*` transliteration not in `<Word>` / `<Term>` | First mention never wrapped |
| Native script (`קרעכץ`, `אהבה`, `doină`) outside `<NativeScript>` / registry | Spelling with no home or speaker path |
| Word in `WORDS` never referenced by `id` / `latin` / alias in TSX or linker prose | Dead registry row, or a page that skips the linker |
| Spoke page with `about` / `answer` / `micro` and no `makeTermLinker()` | Registry loanwords stay plain text |
| New `native.language` missing from `pronunciation-audio.mdc` | Next hunt has no source order |
| Silent `WORDS` row whose “last look” is old or “not searched yet” | Possible clip we never tried |

Skip: English theory terms, catalog artist names (those are `<ArtistLink>`),
quoted lyrics, MIDI / piano-roll copy.

### How to scan

```bash
# Bare italics that may be loanwords (then read — many are English emphasis)
rg -n "<em>[^<]+</em>" src/content src/lib --glob '!**/node_modules/**'

# Known / likely loanword spellings not next to Word/Term
rg -n -i "krechtz|krekhts|doina|doină|freylekhs|freilach|nigun|niggun|hora|horă|sher|bulgar|sârbă|lautari|klezmer|freygish|ahava|davening|mame-loshn|shtetl|tsifteteli|rebetiko|oud" \
  src/content src/lib/catalog src/lib/genres src/lib/scales src/lib/concepts \
  src/lib/cousins src/lib/history src/lib/places src/lib/languages

# Native script dumped into JSX/strings
rg -n "[\p{Hebrew}\p{Greek}\p{Arabic}]" src/content src/lib --glob '!**/words/registry.ts'

# Spoke pages that render registry prose without the linker
rg -L "makeTermLinker" src/app --glob '**/[*]/page.tsx'
```

Read each hit. A second mention of an already-wrapped word is fine.
A first mention in hand-authored TSX that is only italicized is not.

### What to do with hits

1. Register (or reuse) the word. Wrap the first mention.
2. Hunt a **human** clip. Attach only if all three checks pass. Otherwise
   leave `audio` off and update the miss table — do not paper it over.
3. If you add a language, find a clip, or rule a source out, update
   `pronunciation-audio.mdc` in the same change.

Do not finish a scan by adding TTS “just for the words we missed.”
